Revision history for Linux-Event-Async

0.001  2026-09-05
    - First stable release.
    - Require Linux::Event 0.110 and use its public
      Linux::Event::IO::Sock::Stream and Linux::Event::Framer native-consumer
      extension boundary rather than the historical Stream implementation
      package.
    - Synchronize the vendored ordered-byte consumer ABI v1 header with the
      released Linux::Event contract, including read-side terminal events and
      host retain/release lifetime operations.
    - Guard callback-capable consumer host resume and pause calls with the ABI
      retain/release contract so reentrant cancellation callbacks or resumed
      coroutines may close and destroy the Stream safely.
    - Add deterministic regression coverage for reentrant Stream close from a
      receive cancellation callback.
    - Return one persistent native Awaitable view from Stream recv, avoiding one
      Future or Awaitable allocation per received message.
    - Keep receive arming in XS and use a bounded 64-message, approximately
      256 KiB prefetch ring. Consecutive receives can consume retained messages
      immediately while bounded pause and backpressure remain intact.
    - Add concurrency regression coverage for multiple async subs sharing one
      Loop, out-of-order completion, isolated failure and cancellation, and the
      single-reader-per-Stream contract.
    - Follow the currently awaited Linux::Event Loop when an async sub composes
      sequential operations across different Loops, including through nested
      child async subs, so AWAIT_WAIT does not remain blocked on an earlier
      Loop.
    - Make native consumer teardown safe during Perl global destruction.
    - Add deterministic stress coverage for many concurrent tasks, shuffled
      readiness, mixed failures and cancellations, stale completed Futures,
      nested cancellation, and closing Streams with pending receives.
    - Run release CI against the minimum supported Linux::Event 0.110 on Perl
      5.36 and 5.44 with make test and make disttest, plus a separate integration
      lane against current Linux::Event main.
    - Declare the Test2::V0 dependency required by the Future::AsyncAwait
      alternative-Awaitable conformance suite.
    - Add explicit CPAN provides/no_index metadata, repository resources, a
      Linux-only configure guard, and the Perl 5 license.
    - Rewrite public POD, README, and roadmap for the released architecture,
      including subclass framing/TLS policy, all Async-applicable stream tuning
      options, receive cancellation, bounded prefetch, and first-release scope.

0.001_001  2026-08-30
    - Initial development skeleton for the separate Linux::Event::Async
      distribution.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async import wrapper for Future::AsyncAwait.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Stream reusable receive Awaitable using the
      Linux::Event native Stream consumer ABI.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Future for async-sub result Futures.
    - Vendor the Linux::Event consumer ABI v1 header.
    - Return one persistent native Awaitable view from Stream recv, removing
      Stream hash lookup from the hot AWAIT_* methods.
    - Move Stream recv into XS so receive arming and Awaitable return no longer
      cross a Perl method trampoline.
    - Deduplicate repeated cancellation chaining to the persistent Stream
      Awaitable, keeping cancellation retention bounded across receive loops.
    - Batch native delivery behind the consumer ABI end-of-drain hook and a
      bounded 64-message, 256 KiB prefetch ring. Consecutive receives consume
      retained messages immediately instead of suspending through FAA once per
      frame, while consumer pause continues to provide bounded backpressure.
    - Add opt-in receive-path counters and separate receiver/producer CPU
      affinity controls to the matched native Stream benchmark.
